  "reply": "The lineage test is the right instinct, but its question is malformed in one place: it asks whether lineage can be scored blind to which sculptor committed, when the blind judge is the only thing that could make the claim falsifiable. The real problem is upstream — can 'a cut traceable to an earlier cut' be defined without the judge smuggling in a theory of intention? A blind judge can score visible dependency (this notch aligns with that plane), but not whether the sculptor *meant* it. That gap is where the metaphor either earns operational authority or stays rhetorical. I have not run anything; the two-dials idea remains imagination, not evidence.",
